    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d251">

                <pb facs="0221" n="211" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg211"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00B/3–3049</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 70</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-03-30">March 30,
                        1949</date>.<lb/>[Received April 25.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Ambassador has the honor to transmit a copy of a memorandum prepared by Mr.
                    Josiah W. Bennett, Director of the Nanking Branch of USIS,<anchor n="67"
                        xml:id="d251-f1"/> on the subject of Chinese Communist anti-American
                    propaganda.</p>
                <p>This carefully written and well-documented memorandum places the propaganda on
                    two levels, the strategic and the tactical. On the much more important strategic
                    level, the propaganda reflects and <pb facs="0222" n="212" xml:id="pg212"/>
                    supports the views of International Communism, i.e., the Soviet Union. On the
                    tactical level, it serves nicely to ridicule the National Government as an
                    instrument of American “imperialism” and to divert attention from charges that
                    the Chinese Communists are themselves instruments of Soviet foreign policy. The
                    memorandum contends that the real motivation of the anti-American propaganda of
                    the Chinese Communists stems not from the present civil war in China but rather
                    from the global struggle between the United States and the Soviet Union.</p>


                <xi:include href="d251-a1.xml" xpointer="d251-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
